1. Who wrote the *Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen* (1791)?
(A) Mary Wollstonecraft  (B) Hubertine Auclert  (C) Harriet Taylor Mill  (D) Olympe de Gouges
Ans: ^^Answer: (D) Olympe de Gouges^^ — Olympe de Gouges (1791).

2. Mary Wollstonecraft's *Vindication* (1792) answered the ideas on girls' education in:
(A) Rousseau's *Émile*  (B) Locke's *Two Treatises*  (C) Mill's *Subjection*  (D) None of these
Ans: ^^Answer: (A) Rousseau's *Émile*^^ — Rousseau's *Émile* (1762).

3. Who first called herself a "feminist", in 1882?
(A) Olympe de Gouges  (B) Hubertine Auclert  (C) Simone de Beauvoir  (D) Clara Zetkin
Ans: ^^Answer: (B) Hubertine Auclert^^ — Hubertine Auclert (1882).

4. The "wave" metaphor for feminism was popularised in 1968 by:
(A) Rebecca Walker  (B) Gloria Steinem  (C) Betty Friedan  (D) Martha Weinman Lear
Ans: ^^Answer: (D) Martha Weinman Lear^^ — Martha Weinman Lear, "The Second Feminist Wave" (1968).

5. "The personal is political" is the title of an essay by:
(A) Kate Millett  (B) Adrienne Rich  (C) Shulamith Firestone  (D) Carol Hanisch
Ans: ^^Answer: (D) Carol Hanisch^^ — Carol Hanisch (written 1969, published 1970).

6. "Compulsory Heterosexuality and Lesbian Existence" (1980) was written by:
(A) Audre Lorde  (B) Mary Daly  (C) Adrienne Rich  (D) Catharine MacKinnon
Ans: ^^Answer: (C) Adrienne Rich^^ — Adrienne Rich.

7. Who proposed International Women's Day at Copenhagen in 1910?
(A) Clara Zetkin  (B) Alexandra Kollontai  (C) Rosa Luxemburg  (D) August Bebel
Ans: ^^Answer: (A) Clara Zetkin^^ — Clara Zetkin; the UN began marking 8 March in 1975.

8. *Woman and Socialism* (1879) was written by:
(A) Friedrich Engels  (B) August Bebel  (C) Karl Marx  (D) Lewis Henry Morgan
Ans: ^^Answer: (B) August Bebel^^ — August Bebel.

9. Engels' *Origin of the Family* (1884) drew on the anthropology of:
(A) Lewis Henry Morgan  (B) Margaret Mead  (C) Bronisław Malinowski  (D) None of these
Ans: ^^Answer: (A) Lewis Henry Morgan^^ — Lewis Henry Morgan's *Ancient Society* (1877).

10. The Wages for Housework campaign (1972) grew from the work of:
(A) Hartmann and Eisenstein  (B) Dalla Costa and James  (C) Mitchell and Rowbotham  (D) None of these
Ans: ^^Answer: (B) Dalla Costa and James^^ — Mariarosa Dalla Costa and Selma James.

11. "They say it is love. We say it is unwaged work" was written by:
(A) Maria Mies  (B) Silvia Federici  (C) Margaret Benston  (D) Arlie Hochschild
Ans: ^^Answer: (B) Silvia Federici^^ — Silvia Federici (1975).

12. "The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism" (1979), the classic of dual-systems theory, was written by:
(A) Heidi Hartmann  (B) Zillah Eisenstein  (C) Iris Marion Young  (D) Juliet Mitchell
Ans: ^^Answer: (A) Heidi Hartmann^^ — Heidi Hartmann.

13. Juliet Mitchell's *Woman's Estate* (1971) named four structures. Which is NOT one of them?
(A) Production  (B) Reproduction  (C) Socialisation of children  (D) Religion
Ans: ^^Answer: (D) Religion^^ — Production, reproduction, sexuality and socialisation of children.

14. Sylvia Walby (1990) identified how many structures of patriarchy?
(A) Four  (B) Five  (C) Six  (D) Eight
Ans: ^^Answer: (C) Six^^ — Six: paid work, household production, culture, sexuality, violence and the state.

15. Unified-systems theory (1980), in contrast to dual systems, was proposed by:
(A) Sylvia Walby  (B) Heidi Hartmann  (C) Iris Marion Young  (D) Nancy Hartsock
Ans: ^^Answer: (C) Iris Marion Young^^ — Iris Marion Young.

16. Who said "Anatomy is destiny"?
(A) Jacques Lacan  (B) Sigmund Freud  (C) Karen Horney  (D) Nancy Chodorow
Ans: ^^Answer: (B) Sigmund Freud^^ — Freud (1924), adapting Napoleon.

17. *The Mermaid and the Minotaur* (1976) was written by:
(A) Nancy Chodorow  (B) Julia Kristeva  (C) Luce Irigaray  (D) Dorothy Dinnerstein
Ans: ^^Answer: (D) Dorothy Dinnerstein^^ — Dorothy Dinnerstein.

18. The remedy proposed by Chodorow and Dinnerstein is:
(A) Shared (dual) parenting  (B) Separatism  (C) Wages for housework  (D) Legal reform
Ans: ^^Answer: (A) Shared (dual) parenting^^ — Shared parenting.

19. *This Sex Which Is Not One* (1977) was written by:
(A) Hélène Cixous  (B) Julia Kristeva  (C) Luce Irigaray  (D) Simone de Beauvoir
Ans: ^^Answer: (C) Luce Irigaray^^ — Luce Irigaray.

20. Julia Kristeva contrasted the paternal "symbolic" with the maternal:
(A) Imaginary  (B) Unconscious  (C) Real  (D) Semiotic
Ans: ^^Answer: (D) Semiotic^^ — The semiotic.

21. Lyotard defined the postmodern as "incredulity toward":
(A) Science  (B) Religion  (C) Metanarratives  (D) The state
Ans: ^^Answer: (C) Metanarratives^^ — Metanarratives (*The Postmodern Condition*, 1979).

22. "A Cyborg Manifesto" (1985) was written by:
(A) Jane Flax  (B) Donna Haraway  (C) Seyla Benhabib  (D) Sandra Harding
Ans: ^^Answer: (B) Donna Haraway^^ — Donna Haraway.

23. Martha Nussbaum attacked Judith Butler in the essay:
(A) "The Professor of Parody"  (B) "Under Western Eyes"  (C) "The Master's Tools"  (D) None of these
Ans: ^^Answer: (A) "The Professor of Parody"^^ — "The Professor of Parody" (1999).

24. Sojourner Truth's "Ain't I a Woman?" speech was given at Akron, Ohio, in:
(A) 1848  (B) 1851  (C) 1869  (D) 1920
Ans: ^^Answer: (B) 1851^^ — 1851.

25. Crenshaw's example of Black women falling between race and sex claims was the case of:
(A) *DeGraffenreid v. General Motors*  (B) *Roe v. Wade*  (C) *Brown v. Board*  (D) *Dobbs v. Jackson*
Ans: ^^Answer: (A) *DeGraffenreid v. General Motors*^^ — *DeGraffenreid v. General Motors* (1976).

26. "Womanism" (1983) was coined by:
(A) bell hooks  (B) Audre Lorde  (C) Patricia Hill Collins  (D) None of these
Ans: ^^Answer: (D) None of these^^ — None of these — Alice Walker (*In Search of Our Mothers' Gardens*, 1983).

27. "The Master's Tools Will Never Dismantle the Master's House" was written by:
(A) Angela Davis  (B) Alice Walker  (C) bell hooks  (D) Audre Lorde
Ans: ^^Answer: (D) Audre Lorde^^ — Audre Lorde.

28. Feminist environmentalism, which roots women's link to nature in material work, was proposed by:
(A) Vandana Shiva  (B) Françoise d'Eaubonne  (C) Bina Agarwal  (D) Maria Mies
Ans: ^^Answer: (C) Bina Agarwal^^ — Bina Agarwal (1992).

29. *The Veil and the Male Elite* (1987) was written by:
(A) Amina Wadud  (B) Fatima Mernissi  (C) Asma Barlas  (D) Riffat Hassan
Ans: ^^Answer: (B) Fatima Mernissi^^ — Fatima Mernissi.

30. Musawah, a global movement for equality in Muslim family law, was launched in:
(A) 1984  (B) 1999  (C) 2009  (D) 2015
Ans: ^^Answer: (C) 2009^^ — 2009.

31. The poem "We Sinful Women", an anthem of Pakistan's 1980s women's movement, was written by:
(A) Fahmida Riaz  (B) Parveen Shakir  (C) Kishwar Naheed  (D) None of these
Ans: ^^Answer: (C) Kishwar Naheed^^ — Kishwar Naheed.

32. *Huquq-un-Niswan* (1898) was written by:
(A) Sayyid Mumtaz Ali  (B) Sir Syed Ahmad Khan  (C) Qasim Amin  (D) Nazir Ahmad
Ans: ^^Answer: (A) Sayyid Mumtaz Ali^^ — Sayyid Mumtaz Ali of Lahore.
